<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roy Welensky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1991">1991</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roy Welensky was a white Northern Rhodesian politician, born in Salisbury, who rose from railway worker and trade union leader to become prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a> from 1956 to 1963. A leading advocate of the federation, he defended white political dominance while promising gradual partnership with Africans. He could not prevent the secession of Nyasaland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a> and the federation's dissolution in 1963.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roy-welensky.jpg" alt="Roy Welensky" width="206" height="326" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roy_welensky.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raphael Welensky was born on 20 January 1907 in Pioneer Street, Salisbury, the thirteenth child of Michael Welensky, a Lithuanian Jewish trader from near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Wilno</a>, and Leah, born Aletta Ferreira, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a>. His parents kept a poor white boarding house, and his mother died when he was eleven. He left school at fourteen, worked for Rhodesia Railways as a fireman, boxed, and rose to locomotive driver. After the failed Rhodesian Railways strike of 1929 he was moved to Broken Hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, where he chaired the branch of the Rhodesian Railway Workers' Union from 1933.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected to the Northern Rhodesian Legislative Council in 1938, Welensky founded the Northern Rhodesian Labour Party in 1941 to seek amalgamation with Southern Rhodesia, and in 1945 succeeded Stewart Gore-Browne as leader of the unofficial members, while still driving trains. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> rejected amalgamation he worked for a federation instead. At a conference at the Victoria Falls Hotel in February 1949, attended by neither Africans nor the British government, he proposed the Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> as a model and spoke of partnership between black and white, with the whites, he said, as senior partners.</p><p>In 1953 he and Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-huggins">Godfrey Huggins</a> founded the United Federal Party, which won the first federal election; Welensky took more than 80% of the vote at Broken Hill and became Minister for Transport. He succeeded Huggins as prime minister on 1 November 1956.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Welensky backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a>, after which British policy turned to faster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">decolonisation</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a> returned to Nyasaland in 1958 to organise opposition to the federation, and Welensky called the Monckton Report of October 1960, which proposed African majorities in the Nyasaland and Northern Rhodesian legislatures and an option to secede, the death knell of federation. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a> the Royal Rhodesian Air Force evacuated more than 6,000 refugees, and Welensky supported Moise Tshombe's Katanga as a buffer against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a>.</p><p>Under new territorial constitutions the UFP lost each territorial election of 1961 and 1962, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodesian-front">Rhodesian Front</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ian-smith">Ian Smith</a> and Winston Field won Southern Rhodesia. Welensky secured the transfer of federal assets to Southern Rhodesia, and the federation was dissolved on 31 December 1963. He later led the Rhodesia Party against a unilateral declaration of independence, settled at Blandford Forum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a>, in 1981, and died there on 5 December 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roy Welensky” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Southern Rhodesian politicians sought amalgamation to gain the Copperbelt, and Northern Rhodesian settlers, among them from 1941 <b>Roy Welensky</b>'s Northern Rhodesian Labour Party, sought minority rule, alone or with the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>White settler leaders, chiefly Godfrey Huggins of Southern Rhodesia and <b>Roy Welensky</b> of Northern Rhodesia, pressed for a union of the three British territories in central Africa.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harare">Harare</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. Died in Blandford Forum, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.83,31.05&amp;year=1961" aria-label="Harare on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5393 2825 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roy Welensky"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5843 3095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5843 3095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.83,31.05&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
